<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-errors-in-area-approximation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Errors in Area Approximation Under a Curve</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.2 Integration
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Determine errors from using integration and area approximation methods
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Calculating errors between approximate and exact (integrated) areas.
         Suggested activity: Learners compute the percentage error of each approximation compared with the exact integral.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Error between estimate and exact area plotted against n as the slider changes. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core (e). One lesson, flat anatomy.
         Activity: as planning notes (percentage error of each rule against the exact integral, for 2, 4 and 8 strips; group discussion of the pattern) | changed: none
         JSXGraph: fig-error-against-n (assets/jsxgraph/error-against-n.js), as planning notes
         STACK: assets/stack/top/4-calculus/18-integration/05-errors-in-area-approximation/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the error, absolute error and percentage error of an estimate; for polynomials of degree at most <m>3</m>, doubling the number of strips divides each rule's error by <m>4</m> and the mid-ordinate error is half the trapezoidal error, with the opposite sign (nearly so for narrow strips on other curves).</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> compute errors and percentage errors, and predict the effect of more strips.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> find what an error in an estimated land area costs in a sale.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find the two error patterns from a table of estimates.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Half the class works as Akinyi (trapezoidal rule), half as Baraka (mid-ordinate rule). Pool the results in one table on the board, then use the interactive for <m>n = 8</m>. Let groups discuss parts (e) and (f) and report in their own words.</p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-akinyi-and-baraka-compare">
        <title>Akinyi and Baraka Compare</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Akinyi and Baraka, Grade 12 learners in Kisumu, want to know how good their estimates of the area under <m>y = x^3</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 2</m> are. Akinyi uses the trapezoidal rule and Baraka the mid-ordinate rule.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Find the exact area by integration.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\left[\frac{x^4}{4}\right]_0^2 = 4</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Find Akinyi's and Baraka's estimates with <m>2</m> strips and with <m>4</m> strips. Read the values for <m>8</m> strips from the interactive in <xref ref="fig-error-against-n"/>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Trapezoidal: <m>5</m>, <m>4.25</m>, <m>4.0625</m>. Mid-ordinate: <m>3.5</m>, <m>3.875</m>, <m>3.96875</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>For each estimate work out estimate <m>-</m> exact value.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Trapezoidal: <m>1</m>, <m>0.25</m>, <m>0.0625</m>. Mid-ordinate: <m>-0.5</m>, <m>-0.125</m>, <m>-0.03125</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Express the size of each of these as a percentage of the exact value.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Trapezoidal: <m>25\%</m>, <m>6.25\%</m>, <m>1.5625\%</m>. Mid-ordinate: <m>12.5\%</m>, <m>3.125\%</m>, <m>0.78125\%</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>What happens to the errors each time the number of strips doubles?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Each error is divided by <m>4</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Compare Akinyi's error with Baraka's for the same number of strips.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Baraka's error is half the size of Akinyi's and has the opposite sign.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To define the error, absolute error and percentage error, and to state the two patterns.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> parts (e) and (f) are the two error patterns.</p></li>
                <li><p>The percentage error divides by the exact value, not by the estimate.</p></li>
                <li><p>A positive error means an overestimate, a negative error an underestimate.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-errors-in-area-approximation">
        <title>Errors in Area Approximation</title>
        <p>
            When an estimate is compared with the exact value found by integration:
            <md>
                <mrow>\text{error} \amp = \text{estimate} - \text{exact value}</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{absolute error} \amp = \left|\text{estimate} - \text{exact value}\right|</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{percentage error} \amp = \frac{\text{absolute error}}{\text{exact value}} \times 100\%</mrow>
            </md>
            A positive <term>error</term> means the estimate is too big. The <term>absolute error</term> is the size of the error, and the <term>percentage error</term> compares it with the exact value.
        </p>
        <p>
            These are the absolute and percentage errors of Grade 11 (Approximations and Errors), with the exact value from integration in place of the working value; the relative error, absolute error divided by exact value, is the percentage error before multiplying by <m>100\%</m>. The signed error is new: its sign shows whether the estimate is too big or too small.
        </p>
        <p>
            For every polynomial of degree at most <m>3</m>, two patterns hold exactly:
            <ul>
                <li><p>halve the strip width (double the number of strips) and the error of each rule falls to a quarter;</p></li>
                <li><p>the mid-ordinate error is half the trapezoidal error, with the opposite sign.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <note xml:id="note-error-patterns-other-curves">
        <title>Other Curves</title>
        <p>
            For other curves the two patterns are not exact, but they hold very nearly once the strips are narrow.
        </p>
    </note>

    <note xml:id="note-estimates-spreadsheet">
        <title>Using a Spreadsheet</title>
        <p>
            A spreadsheet does the arithmetic for many strips. Put the values of <m>x</m> in column A (start at <m>a</m> and add <m>h</m> each row), the heights <m>y</m> in column B by a formula such as <c>=A2^3</c>, and the weights <m>1, 2, 2, \ldots, 2, 1</m> in column C, and <m>h</m> in cell E1. With rows 2 to 10 (<m>n = 8</m>), <c>=SUMPRODUCT(B2:B10,C2:C10)*E1/2</c> gives the trapezoidal estimate. Change <m>h</m> and the number of rows to change <m>n</m>, and watch the error fall.
        </p>
    </note>

    <figure xml:id="fig-error-against-n">
        <caption>Watch how the percentage errors fall as <m>n</m> grows: doubling <m>n</m> divides them by <m>4</m>.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-error-against-n" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/error-against-n.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-error-against-n" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>The estimates are for <m>y = x^3</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 2</m>, the area in <xref ref="act-akinyi-and-baraka-compare"/>. Crosses show the trapezoidal rule's percentage error and circles the mid-ordinate rule's, for every number of strips from <m>1</m> to <m>12</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Drag the <alert>n</alert> slider. The current <m>n</m> is ringed, and the estimates and errors are shown in the text.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Compare <m>n = 2</m>, <m>4</m> and <m>8</m>. By what number is each error divided when <m>n</m> doubles? What is the smallest <m>n</m> that makes the trapezoidal percentage error less than <m>1\%</m>?</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <p>
        <em>Integration in real life:</em> when land is sold by the square metre, a small percentage error in an estimated area can cost the buyer or the seller thousands of shillings, as the last example shows.
    </p>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-percentage-error-of-an-estimate">
        <title>Percentage Error of an Estimate</title>
        <statement>
            <p>In <xref ref="act-three-ways-to-one-area"/> the trapezoidal estimate of the area under <m>y = x^2 + 3</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 4</m> was <m>34</m>, the mid-ordinate estimate <m>33</m>, and the exact area <m>\frac{100}{3}</m>. Find the absolute error and the percentage error of each estimate.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Trapezoidal: absolute error <m>\left|34 - \frac{100}{3}\right| = \frac{2}{3} \approx 0.67</m>, and
                <md>\text{percentage error} = \frac{2/3}{100/3} \times 100\% = 2\%</md>
            Mid-ordinate: absolute error <m>\left|33 - \frac{100}{3}\right| = \frac{1}{3} \approx 0.33</m>, and the percentage error is <m>\frac{1/3}{100/3} \times 100\% = 1\%</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-predicting-the-error">
        <title>Predicting the Error</title>
        <statement>
            <p>The trapezoidal estimate of <m>\displaystyle\int_0^2 x^3\,dx</m> with <m>2</m> strips is <m>T_2 = 5</m>. Predict <m>T_4</m> and <m>T_8</m> without calculating them.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The exact value is <m>4</m>, so the error of <m>T_2</m> is <m>5 - 4 = 1</m>. Doubling the strips divides the error by <m>4</m>: the errors of <m>T_4</m> and <m>T_8</m> are <m>0.25</m> and <m>0.0625</m>, and they stay positive. So <m>T_4 = 4.25</m> and <m>T_8 = 4.0625</m>, the values found in <xref ref="act-akinyi-and-baraka-compare"/>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-price-of-an-error">
        <title>The Price of an Error</title>
        <statement>
            <p>A plot in Nyeri lies between a straight fence and a river. Measured from one end of the fence, the width of the plot <m>x</m> metres along it is <m>y = 30 + 0.5x - 0.01x^2</m> metres, for <m>0 \le x \le 40</m>, as in <xref ref="fig-nyeri-river-plot"/>. Land here sells at Ksh <m>2000</m> per <m>\text{m}^2</m>.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-nyeri-river-plot">
                <caption>The plot by the river in Nyeri, with its width measured every <m>10\,\text{m}</m>.</caption>
                <image width="80%">
                    <shortdescription>A plot 40 m long between a straight fence along the bottom and a curved river bank along the top. Widths measured every 10 m are 30, 34, 36, 36 and 34 m at 0, 10, 20, 30 and 40 m along the fence.</shortdescription>
                    <description><p>Horizontal axis: distance along the fence, x, from 0 to 40 m. Vertical axis: width y in metres from 0 to 40. The river bank is the curve y = 30 + 0.5x - 0.01x squared. Vertical dashed lines at x = 0, 10, 20, 30, 40 are labelled with the widths 30, 34, 36, 36, 34 m.</p></description>
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=10cm, height=7cm, xmin=0, xmax=44, ymin=0, ymax=42,
  axis lines=left, xtick={0,10,20,30,40}, ytick={0,10,20,30,40},
  xlabel={Distance along the fence, $x$ (m)}, ylabel={Width, $y$ (m)}]
\addplot[pattern=dots, draw=none, domain=0:40, samples=60] {30+0.5*x-0.01*x^2} \closedcycle;
\addplot[very thick, domain=0:40, samples=60] {30+0.5*x-0.01*x^2};
\draw[dashed] (axis cs:0,0) -- (axis cs:0,30);
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:0.3,30.8) {$30$};
\draw[dashed] (axis cs:10,0) -- (axis cs:10,34);
\node[anchor=south, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:10,34.8) {$34$};
\draw[dashed] (axis cs:20,0) -- (axis cs:20,36);
\node[anchor=south, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:20,36.8) {$36$};
\draw[dashed] (axis cs:30,0) -- (axis cs:30,36);
\node[anchor=south, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:30,36.8) {$36$};
\draw[dashed] (axis cs:40,0) -- (axis cs:40,34);
\node[anchor=south, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:40,34.8) {$34$};
\node[font=\small] at (axis cs:20,40) {river};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:20,3) {fence};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find the exact area of the plot.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find the trapezoidal and mid-ordinate estimates with strips <m>10\,\text{m}</m> wide, and the percentage error of each, correct to 2 decimal places.</p></li>
                    <li><p>If the plot were priced from the trapezoidal estimate, by how much would its value be wrong?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>(a) <md>\int_0^{40} (30 + 0.5x - 0.01x^2)\,dx = \left[30x + 0.25x^2 - \frac{0.01x^3}{3}\right]_0^{40} = 1200 + 400 - \frac{640}{3} = \frac{4160}{3} \approx 1386.67\,\text{m}^2</md></p>
            <p>(b) With <m>h = 10</m> the widths are <m>30, 34, 36, 36, 34</m>, so <m>T = \tfrac{1}{2} \times 10 \times \left[30 + 34 + 2(34 + 36 + 36)\right] = 1380\,\text{m}^2</m>. The mid-widths at <m>x = 5, 15, 25, 35</m> are <m>32.25, 35.25, 36.25, 35.25</m>, so <m>M = 10 \times 139 = 1390\,\text{m}^2</m>.</p>
            <p>The absolute error of <m>T</m> is <m>\frac{4160}{3} - 1380 = \frac{20}{3} \approx 6.67\,\text{m}^2</m>, a percentage error of <m>\frac{20/3}{4160/3} \times 100\% \approx 0.48\%</m>. For <m>M</m> it is <m>\frac{10}{3} \approx 3.33\,\text{m}^2</m>, about <m>0.24\%</m>. The boundary bends downwards, so <m>T</m> is too small and <m>M</m> too big.</p>
            <p>(c) <m>\frac{20}{3} \times 2000 \approx 13\,333</m>: priced from the trapezoidal estimate, the plot would be undervalued by about Ksh <m>13\,333</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

    <p>Type exact values as whole numbers, fractions or decimals, for example <c>27/4</c> or <c>6.75</c>.</p>

