    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-percentage-error-of-a-mid-ordinate-estimate">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-percentage-error-of-a-mid-ordinate-estimate" source="stack/top/4-calculus/18-integration/05-errors-in-area-approximation/Percentage-error-of-a-mid-ordinate-estimate.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-errors-in-area-approximation">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-error-trapezoidal-x-squared">
            <statement><p>For <m>y = x^2</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 3</m>, find the trapezoidal estimate with <m>3</m> strips, the exact area, the absolute error and the percentage error.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>T = 9.5</m>; exact <m>9</m>; absolute error <m>0.5</m>; <m>5.56\%</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Ordinates <m>0, 1, 4, 9</m>: <m>T = \tfrac{1}{2}(0 + 9 + 2 \times 5) = 9.5</m>. Exact <m>\left[\frac{x^3}{3}\right]_0^3 = 9</m>. Absolute error <m>0.5</m>; <m>\frac{0.5}{9} \times 100\% \approx 5.56\%</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-error-mid-ordinate-x-squared">
            <statement><p>Repeat <xref ref="ex-error-trapezoidal-x-squared"/> with the mid-ordinate rule.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>M = 8.75</m>; absolute error <m>0.25</m>; <m>2.78\%</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Mid-ordinates <m>0.25, 2.25, 6.25</m>: <m>M = 8.75</m>. Absolute error <m>0.25</m>; <m>\frac{0.25}{9} \times 100\% \approx 2.78\%</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-predict-t4">
            <statement><p>For <m>y = 2x^3 + 1</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 2</m>, find the exact area, the trapezoidal estimate <m>T_2</m> with <m>2</m> strips and its percentage error. Then predict <m>T_4</m> without calculating it.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>Exact <m>10</m>; <m>T_2 = 12</m>; <m>20\%</m>; <m>T_4 = 10.5</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Exact <m>\left[\frac{x^4}{2} + x\right]_0^2 = 10</m>. Ordinates <m>1, 3, 17</m>: <m>T_2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(1 + 17 + 6) = 12</m>, error <m>2</m>, percentage error <m>20\%</m>. The error of <m>T_4</m> is <m>2 \div 4 = 0.5</m>, so <m>T_4 = 10.5</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-spot-the-percentage-mistake">
            <statement><p>Otieno found that an area is exactly <m>50</m>, and his trapezoidal estimate was <m>52</m>. He wrote: <q>percentage error <m>= \frac{2}{52} \times 100\% = 3.85\%</m>.</q> What is wrong? Give the correct percentage error.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>He divided by the estimate; divide by the exact value: <m>4\%</m>.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-kericho-drainage-channel">
            <statement><p>A drainage channel on a tea estate in Kericho is <m>10\,\text{m}</m> wide. Its depth <m>x</m> metres from one side is <m>d = 0.02x(10 - x)</m> metres. Using strips <m>2\,\text{m}</m> wide, find the mid-ordinate and trapezoidal estimates of the cross-section area, the exact area, and the percentage error of each estimate.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>M = 3.4\,\text{m}^2</m>, <m>T = 3.2\,\text{m}^2</m>, exact <m>\frac{10}{3} \approx 3.33\,\text{m}^2</m>; <m>2\%</m> and <m>4\%</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Mid-depths at <m>x = 1, 3, 5, 7, 9</m>: <m>0.18, 0.42, 0.5, 0.42, 0.18</m>, total <m>1.7</m>, so <m>M = 3.4</m>. Depths at <m>x = 0, 2, \ldots, 10</m>: <m>0, 0.32, 0.48, 0.48, 0.32, 0</m>, so <m>T = 1 \times 2 \times 1.6 = 3.2</m>. Exact: <m>\left[0.1x^2 - \frac{0.02x^3}{3}\right]_0^{10} = 10 - \frac{20}{3} = \frac{10}{3}</m>. Percentage errors: <m>\frac{1/15}{10/3} \times 100\% = 2\%</m> for <m>M</m> and <m>\frac{2/15}{10/3} \times 100\% = 4\%</m> for <m>T</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-combining-the-rules">
            <title>Challenge: Combining the Rules (Extension)</title>
            <statement><p>For <m>y = x^3</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 2</m> with <m>2</m> strips, <m>T = 5</m> and <m>M = 3.5</m>. Show that <m>\dfrac{T + 2M}{3}</m> is exactly the area. Explain why this works for every polynomial of degree at most <m>3</m>.</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Use the second error pattern: the mid-ordinate error is <m>-\frac{1}{2}</m> of the trapezoidal error.</p></hint>
            <answer><p><m>\frac{5 + 7}{3} = 4</m>, the exact area.</p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>\frac{5 + 2 \times 3.5}{3} = 4</m>. If the exact area is <m>A</m> and <m>T = A + E</m>, then <m>M = A - \frac{E}{2}</m>, so <m>T + 2M = 3A</m> and the errors cancel. This combination is called Simpson's rule; it is beyond the KICD Grade 12 design.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-errors-in-area-approximation">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give an error table template with columns estimate, exact, error, absolute error and percentage error. Work through <xref ref="example-percentage-error-of-an-estimate"/> and <xref ref="example-predicting-the-error"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-error-trapezoidal-x-squared"/> and <xref ref="ex-spot-the-percentage-mistake"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-error-trapezoidal-x-squared"/> to <xref ref="ex-kericho-drainage-channel"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-combining-the-rules"/>. Extension: how many strips make the trapezoidal percentage error for <m>y = x^3</m> from <m>0</m> to <m>2</m> less than <m>1\%</m>? (The percentage error is <m>\frac{100}{n^2}\%</m>, which is exactly <m>1\%</m> at <m>n = 10</m>, so <m>n = 11</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

